# 7. Employee Access and Permission System

## Problem Statement

An enterprise application provides different access permissions to employees based on their roles. The system must support different implementations of the same access-control contract.

Design the program using the following OOPS structure:

### Interface: `AccessControl`

Create an interface named `AccessControl`.

The interface must contain the following methods:

- `String getRole()`
- `int getAccessLevel()`
- `boolean canAccess(int requiredLevel)`

### Implementation Classes

Create the following implementation classes:

- `Developer`
- `Manager`
- `Administrator`

### Access Levels

| Role | Access Level |
|---|---:|
| Developer | 1 |
| Manager | 2 |
| Administrator | 3 |

The `canAccess()` method must return `true` when the employee’s access level is greater than or equal to the required access level. Otherwise, it must return `false`.

Use an `AccessControl` reference to demonstrate **runtime polymorphism**.

## Input Format

- The first line contains an integer `roleType`.
  - `1` represents Developer.
  - `2` represents Manager.
  - `3` represents Administrator.
- The second line contains `requiredLevel` as an integer.

## Output Format

Print the following:

- Role as a string.
- Access level as an integer.
- Access result as a boolean.

## Constraints

- `1 ≤ roleType ≤ 3`
- `1 ≤ requiredLevel ≤ 3`

## Sample Input

```text
2
3
```

## Sample Output

```text
Role: Manager
Access Level: 2
Can Access: false
```

## Solution

import java.util.Scanner;

interface AccessControl {
    String getRole();
    int getAccessLevel();
    boolean canAccess(int requiredLevel);
}

class Developer implements AccessControl {
    public String getRole() {
        return "Developer";
    }

    public int getAccessLevel() {
        return 1;
    }

    public boolean canAccess(int requiredLevel) {
        return getAccessLevel() >= requiredLevel;
    }
}

class Manager implements AccessControl {
    public String getRole() {
        return "Manager";
    }

    public int getAccessLevel() {
        return 2;
    }

    public boolean canAccess(int requiredLevel) {
        return getAccessLevel() >= requiredLevel;
    }
}

class Administrator implements AccessControl {
    public String getRole() {
        return "Administrator";
    }

    public int getAccessLevel() {
        return 3;
    }

    public boolean canAccess(int requiredLevel) {
        return getAccessLevel() >= requiredLevel;
    }
}

public class Employee-Access-Permission-System {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);

        int type = sc.nextInt();
        int requiredLevel = sc.nextInt();

        AccessControl a;

        if (type == 1) {
            a = new Developer();
        } else if (type == 2) {
            a = new Manager();
        } else {
            a = new Administrator();
        }

        System.out.println("Role: " + a.getRole());
        System.out.println("Access Level: " + a.getAccessLevel());
        System.out.println("Can Access: " + a.canAccess(requiredLevel));

        sc.close();
    }
}
